this fourth of July weekend the country  we broke away from is struggling with  its own independence issues specifically  how to leave the European Union three  years after the brexit vote Britain is  no closer to agreeing to a deal and how  to leave the EU a group of four  campaigners in London has taken it upon  themselves to launch a passionate plea  to remain in Europe shining a very  public spotlight on the politicians who  promised brexit would be easy Debora  patta has more it may not look like the  setting for a hotbed of revolutionary  activity but this innocuous London pub  was exactly where an audacious guerrilla  campaign was born when four young dads  decided to hold politicians to account  for the bricks at chaos nobody was going  back to those politicians as saying hang  on in June 2016 you promised this you  said that you said we hold all the cards  you said we've got the European Union  over a barrel bricks it has already sunk  - Prime Minister's throne favourable  European trade deals into uncertainty  and lifts a nation bitterly divided and  yet three years after the UK voted to  leave the EU it is still no closer to an  agreed exit plan despite the looming  October departure date as James Olly  been and will drowned their bricks at  sorrows a simple plan was hatched why  not throw politicians own words back at  them the day after we vote to leave we  hold all the cards  the idea was to emblazon the quotes on  huge public billboards the only problem  was they had no idea or how to do it the  first night we came out and did it was a  bit of a disaster we didn't really have  the right tools we didn't have the paste  and the right thickness billboards  rounded off with their now infamous  tagline hashtag led by donkeys turns out  there's a lot to be said for laughing at  those in power we feel that by laughing  in them we remove a little bit of their  power as well so we've quite enjoyed  calling them donkeys but nobody was  laughing when their amateur billboards  turned into a nationwide phenomenon  as 1 million people marched in London  earlier this year demanding a second  referendum on brakes it led by donkeys  found themselves hiring helicopters with  the help of crowdfunding to capture a  banner that would be held aloft by the  marchers well could we get a drone oh no  you can't fly drones during this protest  and well no could we get helicopter so  we googled it in the first answer for  helicopters to hire another 5,000 pounds  and we had a helicopter in the air so  yeah I mean it was singularly the most  stressful thing we've done and because  they're pulling out a huge banner like  that over a crowd comes with a lot of  responsibility but actually for me it  gave us one of the greatest returns the  banner became a defining moment of the  day now funded by online donations and  no longer stealing ad space the group  have revealed their identities and have  upped their game with projections in  Brussels as well as poking fun at  international visitors and empowering  isn't it because I think one of the  things is that people are feeling  powerless suddenly these quotes these  promises that were made before the  referendum became a fundamental part of  the debate and I would like to think  that's because we sat here frustrated  printed out a poster and slapped it up  on a billboard side with some cheap  wallpaper paste as for the future I  think all of us generally are optimists  and I don't feel in my heart of hearts  that we are going to leave the opinion  at the end of October while staying in  the EU might be unlikely  it wouldn't be the craziest thing to  happen in British politics over the past  three years for CBS this morning  Saturday Debora patta London
 

